#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292694 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087844 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b18ace11-d31c-5d5a-bd2e-32e1c28af2b9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d05da919-52fc-509f-a8f8-2528fc830fcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291517}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d05da919-52fc-509f-a8f8-2528fc830fcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291517} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd09b302-f2ca-548f-87d3-3d4055d98ae4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292519}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8b59dc04-b4de-517d-b908-41d7f801e2fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-270004" :question/problem "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-9}{2{x}^{3}-2}$ is $x=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-9}{2{x}^{3}-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-9}{2({x}^{3}-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-{3}^{2}}{2({x}^{3}-{1}^{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-3)(x+3)}{2(x-1)({x}^{2}+x+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the difference of cubes formula to factor the denominator.\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\n(x-1)({x}^{2}+x+1)=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $(x-1)=0$ gives the solution $x=1.$\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}+x+1=0$ has no real solutions because the discriminant $D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & ={1}^{2}-4(1)(1) \\\\  & =1-4 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertical asymptote is $x=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402695 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087845 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b0b57da-f9af-5fab-a337-51ee4cb28f9e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes With Common Factors When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b9b1cd4-2765-5911-bf4d-ab94fa28dffe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439745} {:answer/id #uuid "0205637e-c6c7-5efb-b969-574286186336" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439746}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b9b1cd4-2765-5911-bf4d-ab94fa28dffe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439745} :answer-field/id #uuid "701fd38d-2e9d-5fa9-8c52-97331a2dfb74" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440230} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96d20032-2a04-59d8-b369-c2bfeac9edd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439747} {:answer/id #uuid "24389cce-7954-5559-8a48-cb113db6a3b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439748}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96d20032-2a04-59d8-b369-c2bfeac9edd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439747} :ans